//! LiberSystem security package - permissions and capability management.

package liber:security@1;

use liber:base@1.{env-var, error};
use liber:process@1.{start-result as process-start-result, task, task-group};

/// The kinds of capability a component may be declared to receive: a connection to one
/// of the system services. The typed vocabulary a permission manifest is written in - a
/// grant names a service the component may reach, never an ambient right. A component
/// holds exactly the capabilities its manifest lists and can reach nothing else.
enum capability {
	log,
	storage,
	network,
	device,
	config,
	time,
	audio,
	input,
	graph,
	resource,
	process,
	permission,
	supervisor,
	volumes,
	services,
	usb,
	display,
	input-keys,
	audio-stream,
	/// The caller's SessionService client: its jobs, and the right to ask the session to signal
	/// one. It does NOT carry the session's Process handles - `job-signal` acts on the session's
	/// behalf precisely so a tool granted this cannot take them.
	session,
	/// A storage client CONFINED TO THE COMPONENT'S OWN ASSET DIRECTORY, and to nothing else.
	///
	/// An application that ships data beside itself - LiberCommander's syntax descriptors are the
	/// first - needs to read that data and nothing more. Granting `volumes` would hand it every
	/// file on every mounted volume in order to colour some keywords, which is the shape of
	/// over-granting that a capability system exists to make unnecessary: the authority to read one
	/// directory is expressible, so it should be expressed.
	///
	/// The client is minted per launch through `volume-admin.open-directory`, so it carries the
	/// directory as its SCOPE rather than as a convention the holder is trusted to respect: a path
	/// outside it is refused by the service, and the client cannot mint a broader one.
	app-assets,
	/// The authority to RECORD, minted per launch through `audio-admin.open-captures`.
	///
	/// Separate from `audio-stream` on purpose. Playback and capture are the same device and the
	/// same service, and they are not the same authority: "may make a sound" is a nuisance if it is
	/// wrong, "may record" is a microphone. A manifest that could only say `audio-stream` would be
	/// a manifest that hands the microphone to anything that beeps, so the two are named apart and
	/// the connection each mints refuses the other's operations.
	audio-capture,
}

/// A component's permission manifest: its name, the capabilities it requests (the
/// set a packaged component will ship on disk), and the exact set it is granted -
/// the audited intersection of the request with the manager's policy. This typed
/// object is the source of truth for what a component may be granted - never a
/// parsed text / JSON file. A launched component starts with the granted
/// capabilities and nothing else (the sandbox); a requested capability the policy
/// withholds surfaces as an audited denial, and the textual form a tool might
/// render is only ever a view of this object.
record manifest {
	component: string,
	requested: list<capability>,
	grants:    list<capability>,
}

/// One decision in the PermissionManager audit trail: the component a grant decision was
/// made for, the capability in question, whether it was granted, and whether the decision
/// was a runtime permission request rather than a static manifest grant. A `dynamic` entry
/// records a capability the component asked for at launch that its manifest did not declare -
/// decided by the headless policy default and recorded back into the same store - while a
/// static (`dynamic` false) entry is a decision over the component's declared manifest. The
/// policy layer's record of every authority decision, queryable over the typed API the same
/// way the journal is.
record audit-entry {
	component:  string,
	capability: capability,
	granted:    bool,
	dynamic:    bool,
}

/// The PermissionManager contract: the policy over the kernel's capability mechanism.
/// `lookup` returns a component's declared permission manifest (the typed source of
/// truth); `audit` returns the trail of every grant / deny decision the manager has
/// made; `run` is the launcher / granter primitive - it starts a named system tool (a
/// shell command run as its own ELF) under its manifest, granting it exactly its declared
/// capabilities and nothing else, forwards the caller's stdout console, argument string,
/// and inherited working directory to it, and returns the live process handle for job
/// control. The granted clients are transferred to the new process out-of-band over the
/// bootstrap channel the manager drives ProcessService to create; the manager is the
/// launcher / granter, ProcessService the mechanism. A caller (the shell) reaches the OS
/// tools only through this op, never the raw kernel loader.
/// One stage of a pipeline as the caller asks for it: the tool and the argument string it
/// is to receive. The stdio is NOT named here - the broker allocates every edge itself, so a
/// caller can neither choose which endpoint a stage receives nor smuggle one in.
record pipeline-stage {
	name: string,
	args: string,
	/// `2>&1`: this stage's diagnostics go wherever its OUTPUT goes rather than to the terminal.
	///
	/// A stage's stderr is a send-only duplicate of the terminal by default, which is what keeps a
	/// producer's diagnostic out of the pipe its consumer is reading. `2>&1` asks for the opposite -
	/// fold the diagnostics into the stream - and it is the form that most needs the broker, because
	/// only the broker knows which endpoint a stage's output actually is: an edge for every stage
	/// but the last, and the caller's terminal for that one.
	///
	/// A FLAG RATHER THAN AN ENDPOINT, for the reason the record's own comment gives: the caller
	/// names no stdio at all. It says what it wants and the broker decides which handle that is.
	merge-errors: bool,
}

/// What a started pipeline reports back: the process group as ONE capability for job control
/// over every stage at once, and how many stages it covers. One handle rather than one per
/// stage is what makes an eight-stage pipeline expressible in a single reply.
///
/// Deliberately no per-stage koid list yet. Nothing consumes one - the shell drives the whole
/// pipeline through the group - and naming the stages individually belongs with the per-stage
/// completion records, which need a shape of their own rather than a bare list of numbers.
record pipeline-result {
	group: handle<task-group>,
	stages: u32,
}

interface permission {
	@op(1) lookup: func(component: string) -> result<manifest, error>;
	@op(2) audit:  func() -> stream<audit-entry>;
	@op(3) run:    func(name: string, args: string, cwd: string, environment: list<env-var>, @rights(send) stdout: handle<channel>) -> result<process-start-result, error>;
	/// Start a whole pipeline as ONE transaction: every stage is authorized against its own
	/// manifest and created behind the start gate, the edges are allocated by the broker, and
	/// the stages are released together. A failure at any stage starts none of them, because
	/// a half-built pipeline is worse than none - a consumer whose producer never starts
	/// blocks forever. `stdout` is the terminal end the LAST stage writes to; every earlier
	/// stage writes into the edge the broker made for it.
	///
	/// "Starts none of them" rests on `process.release-group`, and it did not use to. The broker
	/// released one koid at a time, so past the first release the transaction could not be unwound:
	/// a stage that had run might already have written, sent or printed, and stopping it afterwards
	/// is not the same as it never having started. The group primitive validates every token before
	/// queueing any thread, so a refusal comes back with nothing started - which is what makes this
	/// sentence a guarantee rather than an intention.
	@op(4) run-pipeline: func(@bound(8) stages: list<pipeline-stage>, cwd: string, environment: list<env-var>, @rights(send) stdout: handle<channel>) -> result<pipeline-result, error>;

	/// Start a program over ONE SELECTED FILE, with an attenuated grant instead of a volume bundle.
	///
	/// The caller names a file it can already reach and the broker mints a client SCOPED TO THAT
	/// PATH - not to the directory it sits in, and not to a sibling - which the child receives in
	/// place of the volumes its manifest would otherwise grant it. So a viewer opened on one file
	/// holds the authority to read that file and nothing else, and cannot reopen the file next to
	/// it or list the directory to find out what those are.
	///
	/// `writable` decides whether the grant may open a transactional writer over the path. It is
	/// the difference between "show this" and "edit this", and it is a decision the CALLER makes
	/// and the broker records, rather than something the target asks for after it starts.
	///
	/// The path is checked against the caller's own reach before anything is minted: this narrows
	/// authority and never widens it.
	@op(5) run-with-file: func(name: string, args: string, cwd: string, file: string, writable: bool, @rights(send) stdout: handle<channel>) -> result<process-start-result, error>;

	/// Start a program AS THE FOREGROUND JOB OF A TERMINAL: the same launch as `run`, and the
	/// caller's terminal control channel with it.
	///
	/// That channel is what lets a full-screen program ASK for raw input and no echo instead of
	/// printing an escape sequence into its own output - where a program's data and its requests
	/// were the same bytes, so `cat` on a file containing them reconfigured the terminal. Without
	/// it `tty_set_mode` answers false and the program runs cooked: it renders, and then every key
	/// meant for it is echoed and swallowed by the line editor. `licoview`, `licoedit`, `lico`,
	/// `imgview`, `less`, `watch` and `ps -i` are all launched this way and none of them could be
	/// typed at.
	///
	/// A SEPARATE OP RATHER THAN AN OPTIONAL HANDLE ON `run`, for two reasons. The op is where this
	/// tree says which KIND of launch is being asked for - `run-pipeline` and `run-with-file` are
	/// the same shape - and a caller with no terminal keeps calling `run`, unchanged. And the
	/// generator emits the `@rights` guard only for a bare `handle<...>` parameter: expressed as
	/// `option<handle<channel>>` the check on this capability would be silently skipped, which is
	/// the one thing a capability boundary must not do quietly.
	///
	/// NOT FOR A PIPELINE STAGE OR A BACKGROUND JOB. The terminal belongs to whoever is in the
	/// foreground, which is the caller's own bookkeeping - the shell sets it with SET_FG - and a
	/// stage nobody is looking at must not be able to put the terminal in raw mode.
	///
	/// `receive` as well as `send`: the child asks the terminal how big it is and reads the answer
	/// back on the same channel. It cannot listen to what the shell and the console say to each
	/// other - this is a channel of the caller's own, not the console's.
	@op(6) run-interactive: func(name: string, args: string, cwd: string, environment: list<env-var>, @rights(send) stdout: handle<channel>, @rights(send, receive) control: handle<channel>) -> result<process-start-result, error>;
}
